From b5182e4dd8e3983d822bc26a1ec2fcb040df9af0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 01:33:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] fix(engine): keep a set of tuples, records or functions
 with a part TLC can't size from collapsing

TLC keeps S \X T, [a : S, b : T] and [S -> T] lazy (SetOfTuplesValue,
SetOfRcdsValue, SetOfFcnsValue), and with them a \cup, \cap or \ it
doesn't reduce. Where a part is a set TLC can't size, such as Int, the
engine held the set as the set enumeration it enumerates to, so
Int \X {} was {}: (Int \X {}) \cap {"a"} was {}, where TLC reduces the
intersection by testing "a" in Int \X {} and fails on a non-tuple, and
PrintT(Int \X {}) printed {}, where TLC's text sizes Int and fails.

The engine now builds such a set nowhere. It enumerates it where TLC
does: a quantifier's bound, a set map, either side of = and the left of
\subseteq. It reduces an intersection or a difference with it to the
other operand's elements it holds, tested in it. An unreduced \cap or \
of lazy sets is irreducible, as TLC's SetCapValue and SetDiffValue are.
Where the set would be held as a value, as an element, a state's value
or ToString's operand, the engine declines, and it declines an equality
whose left value isn't a set, where TLC prints the lazy set.

On the upstream conformance suite, 4 runs that declined now match and
no run changes otherwise. Of 1,200 adversarial specs against TLC, 129
that matched by the collapse now decline; native/design/fallback.md
lists them under the declines to win back.
